# Do and do not lists

Use paired advice lists when a short set of actions and cautions makes guidance easier to follow.

These are civica reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## When to use it

Use them in a help page for maintaining a dashboard or checking an integration. A single instruction usually needs a paragraph, inset or warning rather than a whole list treatment.

## When not to use it

Do not turn status values into advice lists. Do not place essential validation or consent inside this content pattern. Avoid presenting lists side by side, where reading order becomes less clear.

## Home Assistant adaptation

Write advice that the user can act on and that matches verified product behaviour. Keep device-specific servicing instructions in the manufacturer’s guidance. The examples explain interface use and information handling; they do not prescribe physical repairs.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use headings and actual lists. The text of every negative item must contain “do not”, so the instruction stays correct when read without its heading. Tick and cross graphics are decorative and hidden from assistive technology. Colour supports the words only.

